GIT & GITHUB

1. Difference between Git Merge and Git Rebase?
A. Git Merge and Git Rebase are both used to integrate changes from one branch into another, but they handle the commit history differently.

Git merge combines the changes from one branch into another branch while preserving the existing commit history. It may create a new merge commit.
Cmd: git merge <branch-name>

Git rebase moves or replays the commits of one branch on top of another branch, resulting in a cleaner and linear commit history. However, it rewrites the commit history and creates new commit IDs.
Cmd: git rebase <










